## Why This Is Asked

Zero-trust for SAP GUI/RFC in a multi-region, multi-account AWS setup, with password rotation and auditable access, tests real-world security posture and automation.

## Key Concepts

- SSO/SAML integration for SAP access
- JIT access with STS and MFA
- SAP auth mapping to IAM roles and SCPs
- Secrets Manager rotation for SAP passwords
- Private networking with VPC Endpoints
- Observability via CloudTrail and CloudWatch

## Code Example

```javascript
{
  "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
  "Resources": {
    "TempSAPAccessRole": {
      "Type": "AWS::IAM::Role",
      "Properties": {
        "AssumeRolePolicyDocument": {
          "Version": "2012-10-17",
          "Statement": [{
            "Effect": "Allow",
            "Principal": { "AWS": "*" },
            "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
          }]
        },
        "Policies": [{
          "PolicyName": "TempSAPAccess",
          "PolicyDocument": {
            "Version": "2012-10-17",
            "Statement": [
              {"Effect": "Allow", "Action": ["sts:AssumeRole"], "Resource": "*"}
            ]
          }
        }]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this model across many SAP landscapes and tenants?
- What changes would you make to strengthen audit trails for non-repudiation?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical understanding of cross‑account IAM with MFA, least privilege, and IaC integration in a real SAP on AWS scenario.

## Key Concepts

- STS assumeRole with MFA
- IAM trust policies and cross‑account access
- Least privilege for SAP HANA actions
- Terraform for IAM role and trust policy
- CloudTrail auditing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Terraform: IAM role with MFA‑enforced trust
resource "aws_iam_role" "sap_operator" {
  name = "sap-operator"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = { AWS = "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B_ID:root" }
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Condition = { aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent = "true" }
      }
    ]
  })
}
```

```javascript
// Terraform: least-privilege SAP actions
resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "sap_operator_policy" {
  name = "sap-operator-policy"
  role = aws_iam_role.sap_operator.id
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Action = ["sap:Read", "sap:Execute"]
        Resource = "*"
      }
    ]
  })
}
```

```javascript
# CLI test: assume role with MFA and perform a harmless SAP action
aws sts assume-role --role-arn arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_A_ID:role/sap_operator --role-session-name sap-test \
  --serial-number arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_B_ID:mfa/user --token-code 123456
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate ongoing policy least-privilege using AWS Config or IAM Access Analyzer?
- What changes would you make to rotate credentials or rotate the operator role across regions?
